play NES and SNES games

Two more systems on the shelf: the NES on FCEUmm and the SNES on Snes9x
2005, each with its own Games, Labels, Saves and States folder. Upstream
slot has neither, so this is a feature rather than a fix that makes upstream
work here -- it changes slot-ui, and what the player sees, on purpose.

The frame. Upstream copies every core's picture into one 240x160 frame and
centres a Game Boy in it; a 256x224 console picture would lose its edges.
`Platform::canvas()` names the frame per system -- unchanged for the Game Boy
family, 256x224 for the consoles -- and `LibretroCore::set_canvas` sizes the
host's buffer before the game loads. SNES hi-res is averaged pair by pair and
interlace takes every other line, so both land in the same 256x224. The
software game pass already took any source size; it now reallocates its frame
when the size moves, and the GL pass recreates its texture.

The picture. Actual is the largest whole multiple that fits, 2x; stretch
fills the height. L1/R1 switch them on the NES as on a Game Boy. On the SNES
the shoulders are the game's.

Buttons. The SNES reads X and Y from the two turbo bindings and has no turbo;
the emu thread passes its mask through untouched. The NES keeps the Game Boy
layout. The GBA core picker is built from the cores that run a GBA game, not
from every core.

Settings that a television never had -- LCD shaders, colour correction,
bezels -- are hidden in game for both.

The carts are fragilesilver/slot-fragile's (f2da56a): its NES and SNES shells,
`assets/nes_neutral.png` and `assets/snes_neutral.png`, and its media module,
cut to these two systems. GPL-3.0, as slot is.

Nothing here has run on a handheld yet; see notes/nes-snes.md for what to
measure first.

Applies to upstream 91499a9, after 0001-0026.

